= Momentum =

== Overview ==
'''Linear momentum''' is a vector quantity equal to an object's or system's mass multiplied by its velocity.

== Definition ==
For constant mass, linear momentum is written p = mv. Its direction is the same as the velocity, and its SI unit is kg·m/s.

== Scope & importance ==
Momentum provides a powerful description of motion because total momentum is conserved in an isolated system. The momentum form of Newton's second law relates net external force to the rate of change of momentum.

== Core concepts ==
Momentum depends on both mass and velocity. Impulse is the change in momentum produced by a force acting over time. Conservation of momentum is especially useful for analyzing collisions and interactions.

== Examples ==
A slowly moving massive object can have more momentum than a rapidly moving lightweight object. In a collision, the total momentum of an isolated system is the same before and after the interaction even though individual objects change momentum.

== Applications / implications ==
Momentum is used throughout mechanics, collision analysis, rocketry, particle physics, fluid dynamics, biomechanics, and engineering.

== Epistemic notes ==
The elementary expression p = mv is the classical non-relativistic form. Relativistic mechanics modifies the relation between momentum, mass, and velocity at speeds where relativistic effects matter.

